/* The masthead.

   It set the site's palette rather than taking one: cream, champagne, gold,
   and every heading and sentence on every other page is now the same three
   stops. So these read the shared tokens rather than repeating their values —
   changing the site's ink means changing it once, in tokens.css.

   Nothing is lit: no bloom on the letters and no pool of light behind them,
   the same as every other heading on the site now, on a field of flat black. */
.hero {
  --hero-top: var(--title-top);
  --hero-mid: var(--title-color);
  --hero-deep: var(--title-deep);
  /* Still named, because the divider under the name is drawn in it. */
  --hero-glow: 226 206 166;
  --page-glow: 226 206 166;
}

/* The block is centred in the section and the section is the window — but the
   window is not what it has to sit in the middle of. The bar takes the top of
   it and the moon takes the bottom 37%, and what is left is a band running from
   about a tenth of the way down to a little under two thirds. The middle of
   that band is at 36% of the height, not 50%.

   A centred flex item rises by half of whatever is added below it, so this is
   twice the distance between those two: the name now sits in the space it
   actually has rather than in the space the window nominally offers, and the
   gap under the roles is the moon's rather than dead air. */
.hero.hero-pin__page {
  padding-bottom: calc(var(--page-pad-y) + 28vh);
}

/* Unless the window is short, where a lift this size walks the name up behind
   the bar — the type is sized off the width, so a wide, shallow window has a
   full-size masthead and almost nowhere to put it. The moon gives up some of
   its share at the same time. */
@media (max-height: 40rem) {
  .hero.hero-pin__page {
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--page-pad-y) + 10vh);
  }

  .hero__moon {
    height: 30%;
  }
}

/* The moon.

   A band cut off the top of a sphere — see tools/prep-moon.py, which completes
   the disc the photograph only half contains and turns it so the lit limb is
   the edge you see. Stretched to the box rather than fitted to it, which is
   what pins the two ends of the arc to the bottom corners at every window
   shape: taller windows get a rounder moon, wider ones a flatter one, and the
   corners are met either way.

   37% is the rise in the sketch this came from. */
.hero__moon {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  height: 37%;
  z-index: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  background: url('/moon.webp') center top / 100% 100% no-repeat;
}

.hero__stage {
  position: relative;
  /* Over the moon, never under it. */
  z-index: 1;
  width: 100%;
}

/* ---- the name ---- */

.hero__name {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--font-title);
  /* Synthetic bold, for the reason the chapter headings are: Instrument Serif
     ships one weight and the 700 Google answers with is a 400. */
  font-weight: 700;
  /* Sized off the viewport with a ceiling rather than clamped between two
     rems, because the constraint here is not taste but the line fitting: it is
     thirteen capitals that must stay on one line at every width.

     Measured rather than guessed, and the guess was badly out: Instrument
     Serif's capitals are narrow, and the whole line sets in 6.77em. So this is
     the size at which the name lands about 80% of the way across the column at
     1480px, with the rest left for the bloom to fall into before .section
     clips it. The ceiling is what stops a 4K window setting it at 280px. */
  font-size: min(10.6vw, 13rem);
  line-height: 1.04;
  /* Capitals set solid look crowded; a serif's sidebearings are drawn for
     mixed case. This is the smallest amount that opens them. */
  letter-spacing: 0.015em;
  text-align: center;
  /* A name is a signature. It breaks over two lines only if it physically
     cannot fit, which at this size it always can. */
  white-space: nowrap;
  /* No bloom. The chapter headings carry four passes of drop-shadow; this one
     carries none, and is the only heading on the site set that way. At this
     size the type does not need help being seen, and a champagne this warm
     glows on its own against true black — the filter was adding a haze that
     softened the serif's hairlines, which are the whole reason for setting a
     name in this face. */
}

.hero__word {
  display: inline-block;
}

.hero__word + .hero__word {
  margin-left: 0.24em;
}

/* Every letter carries the gradient itself rather than sharing one across the
   heading, and it has to: background-clip on the parent clips to where the
   text is laid out, not to where a transform has since moved it, so a letter
   that rises would have left its own fill behind.

   At 168deg the ramp is all but vertical anyway, so a letter's own ramp and
   its slice of the line's are the same picture — with the bonus that every
   letter now runs the full length of the colour instead of the ones at the
   ends getting a corner of it. */
.hero__glyph {
  display: inline-block;
  background-image:
    /* the travelling highlight. Held at a little over half strength: at full
       it does not read as a light crossing the name, it reads as each letter
       turning white in turn. */
    linear-gradient(
      100deg,
      transparent 16%,
      rgb(255 255 255 / 0.56) 50%,
      transparent 84%
    ),
    /* the fill */
    linear-gradient(
      168deg,
      var(--hero-top) 0%,
      var(--hero-mid) 44%,
      var(--hero-deep) 100%
    );
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-size:
    62% 100%,
    100% 100%;
  /* Parked off the left. A background-position percentage is a percentage of
     the room the image has to move in — here 100% - 62% = 38% of the letter —
     so -190% puts the band's right edge a tenth of a letter clear of it, and
     290% puts its left edge a tenth clear of the other side. */
  background-position:
    -190% 0,
    0 0;
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
  background-clip: text;
  color: transparent;
  -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
}

/* One highlight crossing the name, every nine seconds.

   It is thirteen separate animations and it reads as one because of the delay:
   each letter is lit for a quarter of a second, and the next starts an eighth
   of a second later, which is a band about two letters wide moving at a
   constant speed. Linear on purpose — light crossing a surface does not ease.

   The lead-in clears the entrance: the last letter has landed by 1.5s. */
.hero__stage.is-home .hero__glyph {
  animation: hero-sheen 9s linear calc(2.4s + var(--g) * 0.125s) infinite;
}

@keyframes hero-sheen {
  0% {
    background-position:
      -190% 0,
      0 0;
  }
  2.8% {
    background-position:
      290% 0,
      0 0;
  }
  100% {
    background-position:
      290% 0,
      0 0;
  }
}

/* ---- the line that types itself ---- */

.hero__roles {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  margin: clamp(1rem, 3vh, 2.1rem) 0 0;
  text-align: center;
  font-family: var(--font-display);
  font-size: clamp(0.7rem, 1.12vw, 1.05rem);
  font-weight: 600;
  /* Tracked wide and set in capitals: the site already speaks this way for its
     labels, and under a serif this size a line of ordinary sentence case reads
     as a caption that got lost rather than as the second line of a masthead. */
  letter-spacing: 0.3em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--fg);
}

/* The words as a screen reader gets them — once, punctuated, and not one
   character at a time. The clip rectangle is the standard way to keep text in
   the accessibility tree while taking it off the page; display:none and
   visibility:hidden would take it out of both. */
.hero__said {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  margin: -1px;
  padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;
  border: 0;
}

/* Two layers in one box: the finished line, invisible, holding the width open,
   and the typed line laid over it. */
.hero__line {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  max-width: 100%;
}

.hero__ghost,
.hero__typed {
  display: block;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.hero__ghost {
  visibility: hidden;
}

.hero__typed {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  /* Left, not centre: the typed text has to start where the finished line
     starts, or every keystroke would re-centre what has been written so far
     and the whole line would crawl sideways as it was typed. */
  text-align: left;
}

/* The divider, struck like a key in its turn. Empty inline-block, so it hangs
   from the baseline — the negative alignment is what drops it back down to sit
   with the capitals rather than towering over them. */
.hero__divider {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 1px;
  height: 0.95em;
  margin: 0 clamp(0.55rem, 1.3vw, 1.1rem);
  vertical-align: -0.14em;
  border-radius: 1px;
  background: linear-gradient(
    180deg,
    transparent,
    rgb(var(--hero-glow) / 0.9) 50%,
    transparent
  );
}

/* The caret. It needs no positioning of its own: it is the last thing in the
   typed layer, so it stands wherever the writing has got to, and it steps
   along by the exact width of each character without anything having to
   measure one. */
.hero__caret {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 0.09em;
  min-width: 1px;
  height: 0.95em;
  margin-left: 0.04em;
  vertical-align: -0.14em;
  background: currentColor;
  animation: hero-blink 0.78s steps(1, end) infinite;
}

/* And it goes out a beat after the last character, rather than blinking at the
   visitor for as long as they stay on the page. */
.hero__typed.is-done .hero__caret {
  animation: hero-caret-off 0.45s ease-out 1.1s forwards;
}

@keyframes hero-blink {
  0%,
  50% {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  50.01%,
  100% {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}

@keyframes hero-caret-off {
  to {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}

/* Narrow: the line stays on one line, so the tracking gives way before the
   size does — it is the tracking that costs a third of the width. */
@media (max-width: 34rem) {
  .hero__roles {
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  }

  .hero__divider {
    margin: 0 0.45rem;
  }
}

@media (max-width: 21rem) {
  .hero__roles {
    font-size: 0.6rem;
  }
}

/* Nothing here is load-bearing: the name and the roles are the page and the
   highlight is ornament on top of them. It is gated on .is-home, which the
   component never sets when the visitor has asked for less motion — this is
   the belt to that braces. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .hero__stage.is-home .hero__glyph,
  .hero__caret {
    animation: none;
  }
}
